@meta
id: 25-03
title: Drill — the three lines below
chapter: 25
type: drill
skills: range-syntax
par: 15
minutes: 4
reps: 8
vscode: select three lines with the mouse, Delete
open: jest.setup.ts

@brief
Eight times: the three lines under the cursor are a stale mock that the
new fixture replaced. Delete them without moving.

The cursor is on line 1 and stays there. A range with no address before
the offset means "from the cursor", so `+1` is the line below and `+3`
is three below.

@keys
| `:+1,+3d` | the three lines below the cursor |
| `+1` | short for `.+1` |

@hints
You do not need `.` at all. A leading `+` already counts from the
cursor.
--
`:+1,+3d` Enter, `:wq`.

@solution
    :+1,+3d<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twelve keystrokes. `j3dd` is shorter and it moves the cursor; this does
not. That is the trade the whole chapter runs on — Ex ranges cost a few
more keys and leave you exactly where you were, which is what you want
when the edit is a detour from the thing you were actually doing.

Drill it until `+1,+3` reads as "the next three lines" rather than as
arithmetic.

@start jest.setup.ts
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
jest.mock('../src/infrastructure/services/payment.service');
jest.mock('../src/infrastructure/services/email.service');
jest.mock('../src/infrastructure/services/ledger.service');

beforeEach(() => {
  container.clearInstances();
});

@expected jest.setup.ts
import { container } from 'tsyringe';

beforeEach(() => {
  container.clearInstances();
});
